    "slot_key": "Initial release scope prioritization",
    "slot_value": "Core configuration, monitoring, real-time ingest, basic fault detection, safe recovery behavior, and reliable live operations are priority for the initial release; richer analytics, more advanced visualization, broader integrations, and specialized reports can wait for later phases. Later-phase features should only turn on once the core live path is stable, recovery behavior has been proven, and they have been run against real observing sessions without impacting the correlator or the primary pipeline. Analytics and reporting can come on first if they are read-only and based on trusted output, while visualization and external integrations need extra caution if they could confuse operators or feed other systems with unverified status. Anything that could influence active observing decisions, trigger automation elsewhere, or present incomplete data as authoritative should stay off until the workflow is trusted end to end. Read-only features are acceptable earlier, but anything that writes back, automates decisions, or exposes operational status outside the control room should remain unavailable until the team is confident the data stream is stable and the semantics are settled.",
