    "slot_key": "Controlled and testable operational changes",
    "slot_value": "Changes to configuration logic, recovery behavior, health checks, and any interface operators rely on should be treated as controlled, meaning they need to be testable and reversible before affecting production observing. Monitoring thresholds and data product definitions should also be controlled if changing them would alter alarms or the meaning of the output. The boundary is whether the change can affect observing results or operator decisions in a way that isn’t completely predictable and reversible; if it can, it needs sign-off. Even if a change is partially reversible, that is not enough if undoing it could leave gaps, change timestamps, or make the current observing state ambiguous. Automation can act only when the action is bounded, fully understood, and safe to retry or roll back without affecting the observation. Later-phase features like analytics, reporting, visualization, and external integrations should only turn on once the core live path is stable, recovery behavior has been proven, and they’ve been exercised against real observing sessions without impacting the correlator or the primary pipeline.",
    "operation": "refine"
